course: dictionary
lesson: 13558
title: Impute: blaming and filling in
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Impute is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in serious writing such as reports, news and maths. When you meet a new word, read the words around it for clues. «The teacher imputed the broken window to the boys, although she had no proof.» The clue is "no proof": she blamed them without evidence. So impute means to say that something bad was caused by someone.
explain: Impute often goes with blame, motives or a cause: impute blame, impute motives. Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. «They blamed the failure on poor planning» fits the sentence, so impute means something close to blame or put down to.
explain: In maths and statistics, impute means to fill in a missing number with a sensible guess. «The survey lost three answers, so the scientists imputed them using the average.» The missing numbers are estimated from the others, so the data can still be used.
fact: Impute is a formal verb, so it is most common in serious writing.
fact: «Impute» means to say that something, usually bad, is caused by or belongs to someone.
fact: «Impute blame» means to blame someone for something, often without proof.
fact: In statistics, «impute» means to fill in a missing value with an estimated one, such as the average.
fact: The past form is «imputed», and the -ing form is «imputing».
exercise: «The manager imputed the failure to poor planning.» What does imputed mean here? | *Blamed on, or said to be caused by | Forgot all about | Planned very carefully | Celebrated with a party
exercise: «The survey had three missing answers, so Ravi imputed them using the average.» What does imputed mean here? | *Filled in with a sensible estimate | Deleted all the data | Checked for spelling mistakes | Added the numbers together
exercise: Which is the past form of impute? | imputes | *imputed | imputing | impute
test: «The coach imputed bad motives to the referee, saying he wanted the other team to win.» What does imputed bad motives mean? | Watched the referee run | Gave the referee a present | Asked the referee to explain the rules | *Said the referee had a bad reason for his decisions
test: «The data had gaps, so the team imputed the missing values from the averages.» What is the team doing? | Removing the whole data set | Writing a letter to the scientists | *Estimating the missing numbers from the others | Counting only the gaps
exam: «The council imputed the flooding to the new road, but the report found no proof.» What does imputed most nearly mean? | Thanked the road builders for the flooding | Forgot the road was there | *Blamed the road, saying it caused the flooding | Measured the depth of the flood
